# BUDGEMENT AND PROCURMENT MANAGEMENT

## Purpose

The purpose of this Budget and Procurement Management Plan is to define how financial resources, supplier activities, and procurement processes will be managed throughout the Emergency Department Patient Flow Transformation project.
Effective financial management ensures that the project delivers expected benefits while maintaining cost control, transparency, and value for money.



# BUDGET AND MANAGEMENT APPROACH 

The project has an estimated budget of:

## Total Project Budget

£2.8 million

The budget will be monitored throughout the project lifecycle to ensure spending remains aligned with approved funding.

The Project Manager will work closely with Finance and Procurement teams to monitor:
- Planned costs
- Actual expenditure
- Forecast costs
- Budget variations
- Financial risks


# BUDGET BREAKDOWN

The project budget consists of the following areas:

## Digital Platform Implementation

Estimated Cost: £1.2 million

Purpose: Covers the acquisition and implementation of the digital patient flow management platform.

Includes:
- Software licensing
- Platform configuration
- Vendor implementation support


## System Integration

Estimated Cost: £500,000

Purpose: Ensures integration between the new solution and existing NHS systems.

Includes:
- Data integration
- Interface development
- Technical testing


## Project Resources

Estimated Cost: £600,000

Purpose: Covers project delivery resources.

Includes:
- Project Manager
- Business Analyst
- Technical specialists
- Clinical involvement
- Change management resources


## Training and Adoption

Estimated Cost: £200,000

Purpose: Supports successful user adoption.

Includes:
- Training materials
- Staff workshops
- User support activities


## Contingency Reserve

Estimated Cost: £300,000

Purpose: Provides financial protection against unexpected costs.

Potential uses:
- Additional technical requirements
- Increased resource needs
- Unexpected implementation challenges



# BUDGET MONITORING PROCESS 

Budget monitoring will take place throughout the project lifecycle.

The Project Manager will review:

## Planned Budget

The approved amount allocated for each activity.

## Actual Cost

The amount already spent.

## Forecast Cost

The expected final project cost.

## Variance Analysis

The difference between planned and actual spending.



# FINANCIAL CONTROL PROCESSES

Financial control will follow these steps:

## Step 1: Budget Approval

The project budget is approved through the governance structure.



## Step 2: Cost Tracking

Project expenditure is monitored regularly.



## Step 3: Variance Review

Any differences between planned and actual costs are investigated.



## Step 4: Corrective Action

Actions are taken to control overspending.



## Step 5: Escalation

Major financial issues are reported to the Project Board.



# PROCUREMENT STRATERGY 

The project requires external suppliers to provide digital technology and implementation services.

The procurement approach will focus on:
- Value for money
- Supplier capability
- Security compliance
- NHS requirements
- Long-term sustainability



# PROCUREMENT ACTIVITIES

## Requirement Definition

The project team will define:
- Technical requirements
- Clinical requirements
- Security requirements
- Service expectations



## Supplier Evaluation

Potential suppliers will be assessed based on:
- Technical capability
- Healthcare experience
- Cybersecurity standards
- Implementation approach
- Cost effectiveness
- Support model



## Contract Management

After supplier selection, contracts will be managed through:
- Service level agreements
- Performance monitoring
- Regular supplier meetings
- Issue escalation


# SUPPLIER MANAGEMENT APPROACH 

The Project Manager will maintain effective supplier relationships through:

## Regular Meetings

Review:
- Progress
- Issues
- Risks
- Deliverables



## Performance Monitoring

Supplier performance will be measured against:

- Delivery timelines
- Quality expectations
- Service commitments



## Issue Escalation

Supplier issues will be escalated through the agreed governance structure.



# PROCUREMENT RISKS 

## Supplier Delays

Risk: Supplier may not deliver technology within planned timelines.

Mitigation:
- Clear contract milestones
- Regular progress reviews
- Escalation process



## Cost Increases

Risk: Unexpected supplier costs may affect the budget.

Mitigation:
- Fixed pricing agreements
- Cost monitoring
- Contingency reserve



## Security and Compliance Issues

Risk: Supplier solution may not meet NHS security requirements.

Mitigation:
- Security assessments
- Supplier due diligence
- Information Governance review



# VALUE FOR MONEY ASSESSMET 

The project will demonstrate value for money by:
- Reducing operational inefficiencies
- Improving patient flow
- Reducing delays
- Supporting better resource utilisation
- Improving decision-making

The investment is justified because the expected operational and patient benefits outweigh the project costs.



# FINANCIAL REPORTING 

Financial reporting will include:

## Monthly Budget Report

Including:
- Current expenditure
- Forecast costs
- Budget variance
- Financial risks

## Project Board Updates

Including:
- Overall financial position
- Funding requirements
- Approval requests



# CONCLUSION

Effective budget and procurement management ensures that the Emergency Department Patient Flow Transformation project delivers maximum value while maintaining financial control.
Through structured cost management, supplier governance, and continuous financial monitoring, the project can achieve its objectives within the approved budget.
